NAME
       spectra - spectral decomposition, spectral density, and resonance-time
                 diagnostics in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Spectra provide:
           • spectral decomposition
           • spectral density analysis
           • harmonic distribution
           • envelope correlation
           • diagnostic indicators for triadic stability

DESCRIPTION
       Spectra describe the full frequency-domain representation of resonance-
       time. While frequency identifies components and envelopes describe
       shape, spectra reveal the *complete distribution* of resonance energy
       across harmonic and subharmonic bands.

       Spectral analysis enables RTT systems to:
           • detect instability early
           • select appropriate patterns
           • validate schema compliance
           • diagnose resonance drift
           • maintain multi-agent coherence

       Spectra are computed from triad sampling, lineage history, and
       resonance-state snapshots.

SPECTRAL DECOMPOSITION
       Spectral decomposition breaks resonance-time into constituent frequency
       components. It reveals:

           • fundamental frequency
           • harmonic series
           • subharmonics
           • noise components
           • transient spikes
           • modal clusters

       Decomposition methods include:
           • harmonic decomposition
           • envelope-guided decomposition
           • multi-agent spectral fusion
           • resonance-schema–guided decomposition

       Decomposition is used to identify pattern viability and structural
       integrity.

SPECTRAL DENSITY
       Spectral density measures how much resonance energy is present at each
       frequency. It is the “intensity map” of resonance-time.

       Density types:

           low-density spectrum
                  Sparse energy distribution.
                  → stable, predictable triads
                  → sequential or core patterns

           medium-density spectrum
                  Moderate energy across multiple bands.
                  → spiral, ladder, or weave patterns

           high-density spectrum
                  Energy spread widely; complex structure.
                  → expansion, hybrid, or fused patterns

           noise-dominant spectrum
                  High stochastic content.
                  → requires anchor or mirror overlays

       Density informs coherence thresholds and pattern selection.

SPECTRAL SIGNATURES
       Each triad, operator, overlay, and pattern produces a characteristic
       spectral signature.

       Examples:

           triads
                  Clean harmonic peaks; stable envelopes.

           operators
                  Signature depends on resonance behavior (linear, harmonic,
                  integrative, dissipative, refractive).

           overlays
                  Telescope: widens spectrum
                  Mirror: inverts spectrum
                  Anchor: dampens high-frequency noise
                  Lens: shifts spectral emphasis

           patterns
                  sequential: narrowband
                  spiral: broadband with rising harmonics
                  ladder: clustered harmonics
                  expansion: multimodal
                  weave: interleaved bands
                  constellation: multi-agent harmonic clusters

       Signatures help WRSADC validate structural safety.

SPECTRA AND TRIADS
       Triads act as discrete sampling points for spectral analysis:

           P1 (begin)
               → capture baseline spectrum

           P2 (transform)
               → operator resonance signatures reshape spectrum

           P3 (close)
               → integrate spectral changes into lineage

       Spectral evolution across triads reveals system health.

SPECTRA AND PATTERNS
       Patterns require specific spectral conditions:

           sequential
                  narrowband, low density

           spiral
                  broadband, rising harmonic content

           ladder
                  clustered harmonic peaks

           expansion
                  multimodal, high density

           weave
                  interleaved spectral bands

           constellation
                  multi-agent harmonic clusters

       Pattern morphing often corresponds to spectral transitions.

SPECTRA AND RESONANCE-TIME DIAGNOSTICS
       Spectra provide diagnostic indicators for:

           • resonance drift
           • polarity instability
           • gradient overload
           • envelope fragmentation
           • harmonic desync
           • multi-agent divergence
           • schema violations

       Diagnostic flags include:

           spectral tilt
                  Energy shifts toward high or low frequencies.

           spectral bloom
                  Sudden broadband expansion; potential instability.

           spectral collapse
                  Loss of harmonic structure; coherence failure.

           spectral inversion
                  Mirror-like polarity flip.

           spectral fragmentation
                  Spectrum splits into incoherent clusters.

       WRSADC uses these indicators to prevent unsafe dispatch.

SPECTRA IN MULTI-AGENT CONSTELLATIONS
       Constellations require spectral compatibility:

           • shared harmonic clusters
           • aligned spectral envelopes
           • compatible density profiles
           • stable coupling ratios

       Spectral divergence leads to desync or fragmentation.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Schemas define:
           • allowed spectral ranges
           • harmonic constraints
           • density limits
           • decomposition rules

       Spectra must satisfy:
           • resonance-schema
           • structural-schema
           • constellation-schema

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • spectral snapshots
           • decomposition history
           • density evolution
           • harmonic transitions
           • diagnostic events

       Spectral diagnostics depend on lineage continuity.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes spectral decomposition
           • validates spectral-schema compliance
           • selects patterns based on spectral stability
           • applies overlays to correct spectral imbalance
           • blocks unsafe spectral states

       WRSADC is the spectral diagnostician.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       The Engine:
           • executes spectral-sensitive operators
           • updates internal RT-spectrum
           • integrates spectral effects

       The Engine assumes spectral safety; WRSADC guarantees it.

NOTES
       Spectra are the diagnostic backbone of RTT. They reveal the hidden
       structure of resonance-time, guiding safe triadic execution, adaptive
       pattern selection, and multi-agent coherence across the entire system.

